For a typical **I²C SSD1306 OLED** and ESP32, connect:

| OLED | ESP32 |
|---|---|
| VCC | 3.3 V |
| GND | GND |
| SDA | GPIO 21 |
| SCL | GPIO 22 |

Use the **Adafruit SSD1306** and **Adafruit GFX** libraries. A basic example for a 128×64 display:

```cpp
#include <Wire.h>
#include <Adafruit_GFX.h>
#include <Adafruit_SSD1306.h>

#define SCREEN_WIDTH 128
#define SCREEN_HEIGHT 64

Adafruit_SSD1306 display(
  SCREEN_WIDTH,
  SCREEN_HEIGHT,
  &Wire,
  -1                 // Reset pin; use the GPIO number if your module has one
);

void setup() {
  Wire.begin(21, 22);

  // 0x3C is most common; some displays use 0x3D
  if (!display.begin(SSD1306_SWITCHCAPVCC, 0x3C)) {
    while (true);
  }

  display.clearDisplay();
  display.setTextSize(1);
  display.setTextColor(SSD1306_WHITE);
  display.setCursor(0, 0);
  display.println("Hello, ESP32!");
  display.display();
}

void loop() {
}
```

If the display does not initialize, try address `0x3D` or use an I²C scanner to find its address. Confirm that your particular OLED breakout supports 3.3 V; ESP32 GPIOs are not 5 V tolerant.

